"""Scene definitions for the Workload Identity teaching video (generated from concept.toml)."""

SCENES = [
    dict(key='01-poster', kind='poster', title='Workload Identity',
         body=None,
         narration='Hello, and welcome. [[slnc 400]] This video explains workload identity, with SPIFFE and SPIRE. [[slnc 300]] This video is presented by Jayasekhar Konduru. [[slnc 600]] First, a simple definition. [[slnc 300]] A shared secret says only that someone who holds this key is calling, never which service. [[slnc 300]] Workload identity gives each service its own name, a SPIFFE I D, proven by a short lived certificate that the platform hands out and renews. [[slnc 300]] The service never stores a secret. [[slnc 600]] Think of one master key shared by every branch, against a daily badge printed for each person at the door. [[slnc 600]] Our example is a bank.'),
    dict(key='02-scenario', kind='bullets', title='The Scenario',
         body=["A bank's ledger is called by payments", 'and reporting.', '', 'Before: one static secret copied into 3 services.', 'After: a real SPIRE server and agent give each', 'a SPIFFE ID and a certificate that lives 20 seconds.'],
         narration="Here is the scenario. [[slnc 400]] A bank's ledger service is called by payments, and by reporting. [[slnc 500]] Before: one static secret is copied into three services, and the ledger takes the caller's word for who it is. [[slnc 500]] After: a real SPIRE server and agent give each workload a SPIFFE I D, and an X 5 0 9 certificate, called an S V I D, that lives twenty seconds."),
    dict(key='03-words', kind='bullets', title='The Words',
         body=['Workload: a running service, such as payments', "SPIFFE ID: a workload's name, spiffe://bank.example/...", 'SVID: a short-lived certificate that carries that name', 'SPIRE server: keeps the entries, signs the SVIDs', 'SPIRE agent: checks who a process is, hands it its SVID', 'Registration entry: a selector and the ID it gets'],
         narration="Before the demos, the words. [[slnc 500]] A workload is a running service, such as payments. [[slnc 500]] A SPIFFE I D is a workload's name, written like spiffe colon slash slash bank dot example slash payments. [[slnc 500]] An S V I D is a short lived certificate that carries that name. [[slnc 500]] The SPIRE server keeps the registration entries and signs the S V I Ds. [[slnc 500]] The SPIRE agent runs beside the workloads: it checks who a process really is, and hands it its S V I D. [[slnc 500]] And a registration entry pairs a selector, here the operating system user, with the I D that goes with it."),
    dict(key='04-letters-1', kind='bullets', title='What the Letters Stand For',
         body=['SPIFFE: Secure Production Identity Framework For Everyone: the naming standard', 'SPIRE: the SPIFFE Runtime Environment: a server and agents that issue SVIDs', 'SVID: SPIFFE Verifiable Identity Document: here an X.509 certificate', "ID: identifier: the workload's name", 'X.509: the standard format of certificates', 'TLS, mTLS: Transport Layer Security; mutual TLS: both ends show a certificate'],
         narration="A few letters come up again and again, so here is what they stand for. [[slnc 400]] SPIFFE is the secure production identity framework for everyone: the standard for workload names. [[slnc 400]] SPIRE is the SPIFFE runtime environment: a server and agents that issue the certificates. [[slnc 400]] S V I D is SPIFFE verifiable identity document. Here it is an X five oh nine certificate that carries the SPIFFE I D. [[slnc 400]] I D is identifier: the workload's name. [[slnc 400]] X five oh nine is the standard format of certificates. [[slnc 400]] T L S is transport layer security. Mutual T L S means both ends show a certificate and check the other's."),
    dict(key='05-letters-2', kind='bullets', title='What the Letters Stand For (continued)',
         body=['TTL: Time To Live: how long an SVID is valid', "API: Application Programming Interface: the Workload API is the agent's local socket", 'HTTP: Hypertext Transfer Protocol: 200 OK, 401 no valid key, 403 Forbidden', 'uid: user identifier: the operating system user number, 2001 to 2004 here', 'OK: the answer of a request that worked (HTTP 200)'],
         narration="T T L is time to live: how long an S V I D is valid. [[slnc 400]] A P I is application programming interface. The workload A P I is the agent's local socket that hands out S V I Ds. [[slnc 400]] H T T P is the hypertext transfer protocol. Two hundred is O K, four oh one means no valid key, four oh three means forbidden. [[slnc 400]] U I D is the user identifier: the operating system's number for a user. [[slnc 400]] O K is the answer of a request that worked."),
    dict(key='06-who', kind='figure', title="Who decides a workload's name",
         body='who_decides',
         narration="Let's see the core idea on one example. [[slnc 400]] On the left, the input: the reporting process, user two thousand and three, which claims to be payments. [[slnc 300]] It asks the SPIRE agent for its identity, through a local socket. [[slnc 600]] In the middle, how the agent decides. [[slnc 300]] It does not read the claim. [[slnc 300]] It asks the operating system which user owns the process on the other end of the socket: two thousand and three. [[slnc 300]] Then it finds the registration entry with that selector. [[slnc 600]] On the right, the result: spiffe colon slash slash bank dot example slash reporting, in a certificate that lives twenty seconds. [[slnc 300]] The claim changed nothing. [[slnc 300]] The platform decides who a workload is, not the workload."),
    dict(key='07-act-1-figure', kind='figure', title="One key, and the caller's own word",
         body='shared_secret',
         narration="First demo: the before. [[slnc 400]] At the top, the input: one secret, copied into three services. [[slnc 300]] The ledger checks the key, and then reads a header in which the caller says who it is. [[slnc 700]] In the middle, the requests from inside the bank. [[slnc 300]] Reporting may read statements, and gets two hundred. [[slnc 300]] When reporting asks for a transfer under its own name, it gets four oh three. [[slnc 300]] But when it simply says it is payments, it gets two hundred. [[slnc 700]] At the bottom, the leak. [[slnc 300]] Fraud engine's configuration file reaches an attacker outside the bank. [[slnc 300]] With that copy, the attacker is payments, and reporting, and anyone else. [[slnc 300]] Only a wrong key is refused, with four oh one. [[slnc 300]] The key says someone who holds it is calling, never which service."),
    dict(key='08-act-1-1', kind='console', title='Act One — One shared secret in three services',
         body="ONE. One shared secret in three services.\n  THE SECRET (24 random bytes, never printed):\n    copied into payments, reporting, fraud-engine: 3 of 3\n  THE LEDGER'S RULES:\n    spiffe://bank.example/payments    POST /transfers\n    spiffe://bank.example/reporting   GET /statements\n  THE REQUESTS (the right key; X-Service says who calls):\n    caller     says       request          answer\n    reporting  reporting  GET /statements  200\n    payments   payments   POST /transfers  200\n    reporting  reporting  POST /transfers  403\n    reporting  payments   POST /transfers  200\n    the ledger takes the caller's word for who it is",
         narration="Here is the real output. [[slnc 400]] The secret: copied into three of three services. [[slnc 300]] The ledger's two rules. [[slnc 400]] The requests: reporting reads statements, two hundred; payments transfers, two hundred; reporting transfers, four oh three. [[slnc 300]] But reporting saying payments: two hundred. [[slnc 300]] The ledger takes the caller's word."),
    dict(key='09-act-1-2', kind='console', title='Act One — One shared secret in three services',
         body="ONE. (continued)\n  THE LEAK (fraud-engine's configuration, outside the bank):\n    caller     says       request          answer\n    attacker   payments   POST /transfers  200\n    attacker   reporting  GET /statements  200\n    wrong key  payments   POST /transfers  401\n    with the one secret, the attacker is every service",
         narration='The leak. [[slnc 300]] The attacker says payments, and transfers: two hundred. [[slnc 300]] Says reporting, and reads statements: two hundred. [[slnc 300]] Only a wrong key gets four oh one.'),
    dict(key='10-act-2-figure', kind='figure', title="A SPIFFE ID and an SVID's dates, part by part",
         body='svid_parts',
         narration="Second demo, the result in detail. [[slnc 400]] At the top, one SPIFFE I D taken apart. [[slnc 300]] The scheme, spiffe colon slash slash. [[slnc 300]] The trust domain, bank dot example: who vouches for the name. [[slnc 300]] And the path, slash reporting: the workload's name inside that domain. [[slnc 700]] At the bottom, the S V I D's dates. [[slnc 300]] From not before to not after is thirty seconds. [[slnc 300]] SPIRE starts the certificate ten seconds early, so a clock that is a little behind still accepts it. [[slnc 300]] Thirty minus ten is twenty seconds: the time to live we asked for. [[slnc 700]] So the certificate that proves this name is worthless twenty seconds after it is issued, and the agent must hand out the next one."),
    dict(key='11-act-2-1', kind='console', title='Act Two — SPIRE decides who a workload is',
         body='TWO. SPIRE decides who a workload is.\n  THE SETUP (server and agent in one Linux container):\n    SPIRE          1.15.5 (linux arm64; no macOS build exists)\n    trust domain   bank.example\n    entries        3, chosen by the user a process runs as\n  THE REGISTRATION ENTRIES (selector -> SPIFFE ID):\n    unix:uid:2001  ->  spiffe://bank.example/ledger\n    unix:uid:2002  ->  spiffe://bank.example/payments\n    unix:uid:2003  ->  spiffe://bank.example/reporting\n  THE SAME PROGRAM, RUN AS EACH USER (the SVID it got):\n    user 2001                  spiffe://bank.example/ledger\n    user 2002                  spiffe://bank.example/payments\n    user 2003                  spiffe://bank.example/reporting\n    user 2003, says payments   spiffe://bank.example/reporting',
         narration="The setup: SPIRE one fifteen five, the trust domain, and three entries. [[slnc 400]] The registration entries: a user number on the left, a SPIFFE I D on the right. [[slnc 400]] The same program, run as each user, gets that user's I D. [[slnc 300]] Run as user two thousand and three while saying it is payments, it still gets reporting."),
    dict(key='12-act-2-2', kind='console', title='Act Two — SPIRE decides who a workload is',
         body='TWO. (continued)\n  ONE SPIFFE ID, PART BY PART:\n    scheme         spiffe://\n    trust domain   bank.example\n    path           /reporting\n  HOW LONG AN SVID LIVES (from its own dates):\n    Not After - Not Before     30 s\n    minus 10 s back-dating     30 - 10 = 20 s, the TTL\n    left when just issued      at most 20 s: yes',
         narration='One SPIFFE I D, part by part: scheme, trust domain, path. [[slnc 400]] How long an S V I D lives: thirty seconds on the certificate, minus ten seconds back dated, is twenty seconds, the time to live.'),
    dict(key='13-act-3-figure', kind='figure', title="Both ends check the other's SPIFFE ID",
         body='mutual_check',
         narration="Third demo: two Java services that check each other. [[slnc 400]] At the top, the requests. [[slnc 300]] The ledger no longer reads a header. [[slnc 300]] It reads the SPIFFE I D in the caller's certificate, and applies the same two rules as before. [[slnc 300]] Payments may transfer, and gets four oh three for statements; reporting the other way round. [[slnc 700]] At the bottom, two impostors. [[slnc 300]] On the left, a server run by reporting, pretending to be the ledger. [[slnc 300]] Payments checks the server's I D too, expects the ledger, and refuses. [[slnc 300]] On the right, a certificate the attacker made himself, with the payments name written in it. [[slnc 300]] No SPIRE signature, so the handshake fails, and zero requests reach the ledger's code."),
    dict(key='14-act-3-1', kind='console', title="Act Three — Two Java services check each other's SPIFFE ID",
         body="THREE. Two Java services check each other's SPIFFE ID.\n  THE REQUESTS (mutual TLS; the ID comes from the certificate):\n    caller     request          answer\n    payments   POST /transfers  200\n    payments   GET /statements  403\n    reporting  GET /statements  200\n    reporting  POST /transfers  403\n  THE LEDGER'S AUDIT (4 requests, named by the certificate):\n    spiffe://bank.example/payments\n    spiffe://bank.example/reporting",
         narration="The requests over mutual T L S: payments transfers, two hundred; payments reads statements, four oh three; reporting reads statements, two hundred; reporting transfers, four oh three. [[slnc 400]] The ledger's audit names four requests, by the I D in the certificate."),
    dict(key='15-act-3-2', kind='console', title="Act Three — Two Java services check each other's SPIFFE ID",
         body="THREE. (continued)\n  TWO IMPOSTORS:\n    a server run as reporting, called as the ledger: refused\n      payments expects spiffe://bank.example/ledger\n    a self-made certificate naming payments: refused\n      requests that reached the ledger's code: 0",
         narration='Two impostors: a fake ledger run by reporting, refused; a self made certificate naming payments, refused, and nothing reached the ledger.'),
    dict(key='16-act-4-figure', kind='figure', title='Fifty seconds of calls, and SVIDs that live twenty',
         body='rotation_timeline',
         narration="Fourth demo: rotation. [[slnc 400]] At the top, the input: payments calls the ledger four times a second, on a new connection each time, for fifty seconds. [[slnc 300]] Each certificate lives only twenty seconds, so fifty seconds is two and a half lifetimes. [[slnc 700]] The time line shows it. [[slnc 300]] No single certificate could last the test. [[slnc 300]] The agent hands out a new one before the old one ends, and the next handshake simply uses it. [[slnc 700]] At the bottom, the result. [[slnc 300]] At least a hundred calls, none failed. [[slnc 300]] Both certificates, payments' own and the ledger's, were replaced at least three times. [[slnc 300]] And neither service has a line of renewal code."),
    dict(key='17-act-4', kind='console', title='Act Four — Rotation: an SVID that lives 20 seconds',
         body="FOUR. Rotation: an SVID that lives 20 seconds.\n  THE TEST (payments calls the ledger, new connection each):\n    for            50 s, about 4 calls a second\n    SVID lifetime  20 s, so 50 / 20 = 2.5 lifetimes\n  THE RESULT:\n    calls, at least 100        yes\n    calls that failed          0\n    payments' SVID replaced    at least 3 times: yes\n    the ledger's, as seen      at least 3 times: yes\n    renewal code in services   none\n    the Workload API client swaps the SVID; each TLS\n    handshake reads the current one",
         narration="The test: fifty seconds, about four calls a second; the S V I D lives twenty seconds, so two and a half lifetimes. [[slnc 400]] The result: at least a hundred calls, zero failed. [[slnc 300]] Payments' S V I D replaced at least three times, and the ledger's too. [[slnc 300]] No renewal code in the services: the workload A P I client swaps the S V I D, and each handshake reads the current one."),
    dict(key='18-act-5-figure', kind='figure', title='The stolen copy against the clock',
         body='stolen_timeline',
         narration="Fifth demo: a stolen S V I D. [[slnc 400]] The input: the files a helper left on payments' disk, the certificate, its private key and the bundle, copied by an attacker. [[slnc 700]] On the time line, the result. [[slnc 300]] At once, the copy works: two hundred, and the ledger's audit names payments. [[slnc 300]] A stolen certificate is as good as the original, while it lives. [[slnc 300]] Twenty one seconds later, past its twenty second lifetime, the same copy is refused. [[slnc 700]] On the right, the same moment. [[slnc 300]] The real payments, holding a fresh certificate, still gets two hundred. [[slnc 300]] And the shared secret that leaked in the first demo still works too, and will until someone changes it."),
    dict(key='19-act-5', kind='console', title='Act Five — A stolen SVID',
         body="FIVE. A stolen SVID.\n  THE THEFT (files an SVID helper left on payments' disk):\n    certificate, private key, bundle: copied by another user\n  THE ATTACKER'S CALLS (POST /transfers, with the copy):\n    at once            200\n    the audit names    spiffe://bank.example/payments\n    after 21 s (> 20)  refused\n  AT THE SAME MOMENT:\n    payments itself            200\n    the leaked shared secret   200",
         narration="The theft: certificate, private key and bundle, copied by another user. [[slnc 400]] The attacker's calls: two hundred at once, and the audit names payments. [[slnc 300]] After twenty one seconds: refused. [[slnc 400]] At the same moment, payments itself gets two hundred, and the leaked shared secret still gets two hundred."),
    dict(key='20-act-6-figure', kind='figure', title='No entry, no identity',
         body='no_entry',
         narration="Sixth demo: a process that matches no entry. [[slnc 400]] At the top, the input: a process run as the attacker's own user, two thousand and four, that claims to be payments. [[slnc 300]] The agent looks it up: no entry selects that user. [[slnc 300]] So no S V I D is issued, and with no certificate it cannot even start a handshake with the ledger. [[slnc 700]] At the bottom, an entry is taken away. [[slnc 300]] The operator deletes the payments entry on the server. [[slnc 300]] A new payments process now gets nothing either. [[slnc 300]] Reporting, which still has its entry, carries on with two hundred. [[slnc 600]] Identity is something the platform gives, and can take back, not something a process can claim."),
    dict(key='21-act-6', kind='console', title='Act Six — A workload that matches no entry',
         body="SIX. A workload that matches no entry.\n  THE ATTACKER'S OWN USER (2004), SAYING IT IS PAYMENTS:\n    asks for an SVID            no SVID issued\n    calls the ledger            no SVID issued\n    reached the ledger's code   0\n  THE PAYMENTS ENTRY IS DELETED (checked within 40 s):\n    a new payments process      no SVID issued\n    reporting, GET /statements  200",
         narration="The attacker's own user, saying it is payments: no S V I D issued, the call to the ledger has none either, and zero requests reached the ledger's code. [[slnc 400]] The payments entry is deleted: a new payments process gets no S V I D. [[slnc 300]] Reporting is unaffected: two hundred."),
    dict(key='22-act-7-figure', kind='figure', title='What it takes to evict the attacker',
         body='eviction',
         narration='Last demo: getting the attacker out. [[slnc 400]] On the left, the shared secret. [[slnc 300]] The ledger changes it, and the leaked copy is refused with four oh one. [[slnc 300]] But all three services held the same secret, so all three of three now fail too, until each one is given the new value. [[slnc 300]] Evicting one attacker means an outage and a rollout. [[slnc 700]] On the right, the stolen S V I D. [[slnc 300]] Nobody changed anything. [[slnc 300]] Zero services touched: it expired by itself after twenty seconds. [[slnc 300]] Meanwhile the leaked secret kept working, two hundred, until it was changed. [[slnc 600]] A short lifetime is the revocation that always works.'),
    dict(key='23-act-7', kind='console', title='Act Seven — Evicting the attacker',
         body='SEVEN. Evicting the attacker.\n  THE SHARED SECRET IS CHANGED:\n    the leaked copy        401\n    services that fail     3 of 3, until each gets the new one\n  THE STOLEN SVID:\n    services changed       0: it expired by itself\n    the leaked secret      200 until it was changed',
         narration='The shared secret is changed: the leaked copy gets four oh one, and three of three services fail until each gets the new one. [[slnc 400]] The stolen S V I D: zero services changed, it expired by itself. [[slnc 300]] The leaked secret had worked, two hundred, until it was changed.'),
    dict(key='24-costs', kind='bullets', title='What It Costs',
         body=['A SPIRE server and an agent on every node', 'Registration entries must be kept right', 'A stolen SVID works until it expires', 'The SPIRE server is now a target', 'Services need a Workload API client'],
         narration='Nothing is free. [[slnc 400]] Here is what it costs. [[slnc 500]] You run a SPIRE server, and an agent on every node. [[slnc 500]] Registration entries must be kept right, because they decide who gets which name. [[slnc 500]] A stolen S V I D still works until it expires. [[slnc 500]] The SPIRE server is now a target worth protecting. [[slnc 300]] And services need a workload A P I client, and a T L S setup that picks up new certificates without a restart.'),
    dict(key='25-roles', kind='bullets', title='Who Does What',
         body=['LegacyLedger: the before, one shared secret', 'Lab: a real SPIRE server and agent', 'Workload: gets its identity from the agent', 'Policy: which SPIFFE ID may call which route', 'SpiffeIds, Rotations: an ID taken apart, rotations', 'WorkloadIdentityDemo: runs the demos'],
         narration="Here is who does what in the code. [[slnc 400]] LegacyLedger is the before: one shared secret, and the caller's own word. [[slnc 500]] Lab runs a real SPIRE server and agent in a Linux container. [[slnc 500]] Workload is the Java program each service runs. [[slnc 300]] It never says who it is: its identity is whatever the agent hands it. [[slnc 500]] Policy says which SPIFFE I D may call which route. [[slnc 300]] SpiffeIds takes an I D apart, and Rotations counts how often a certificate was replaced. [[slnc 300]] And WorkloadIdentityDemo runs the demos."),
    dict(key='26-recognise', kind='bullets', title='Where You Have Seen It',
         body=['An API key in an environment variable,', '  the same in three services', 'An ID such as spiffe://bank.example/payments', 'A certificate that expires within the hour', 'A header such as X-Service-Name: payments'],
         narration="You have probably met this already. [[slnc 400]] The same A P I key in an environment variable of three services is a shared secret. [[slnc 500]] An I D starting spiffe, colon, slash slash, is a workload identity. [[slnc 500]] A certificate that expires within the hour is a short lived S V I D. [[slnc 500]] And a header that says which service is calling is the caller's own word, which proves nothing."),
    dict(key='27-verdict', kind='bullets', title='How To Use It',
         body=['Give each service its own identity', 'Let the platform prove it, not the service', 'Keep certificates short-lived and rotated', 'Authorize by the SPIFFE ID', 'Retire the shared secrets'],
         narration='So, how do you use it? [[slnc 400]] Give each service its own identity. [[slnc 500]] Let the platform prove who a process is, not the process itself. [[slnc 500]] Keep certificates short lived, and rotated automatically. [[slnc 500]] Authorize by the SPIFFE I D. [[slnc 300]] And retire the shared secrets once nothing uses them.'),
    dict(key='28-classes', kind='bullets', title='The Java Classes',
         body=["LegacyLedger: the before: one shared secret and the caller's word", 'Lab: a real SPIRE server and agent in a Linux container', 'Workload: the Java program each service runs', 'Policy: which SPIFFE ID may call which route', 'SpiffeIds: a teaching class: a SPIFFE ID taken apart by hand', 'Rotations: how often a certificate was replaced'],
         narration='Now, a quick look at the code. [[slnc 300]] SPIRE is real; the classes set it up, run the workloads as different users, and check what they get. [[slnc 300]] Here are the ones that matter.'),
    dict(key='29-class-1', kind='code', title='LegacyLedger — the key, then the claimed name',
         body="// LegacyLedger: the before: one shared secret and the caller's word\nif (presentedKey == null || !presentedKey.equals(secret)) {\n    return 401;\n...\nreturn Policy.allows(Policy.DOMAIN + claimedService, method, path) ? 200 : 403;",
         narration='The key proves only that the caller holds the key. [[slnc 300]] After that, the name the caller claims is all there is to check.'),
    dict(key='30-class-2', kind='code', title='Lab — a registration entry: a user number and an ID',
         body='// Lab: a real SPIRE server and agent in a Linux container\nserver("entry", "create", "-parentID", NODE,\n        "-spiffeID", "spiffe://" + DOMAIN + "/" + workload,\n        "-selector", "unix:uid:" + UIDS.get(workload),\n        "-x509SVIDTTL", Integer.toString(SVID_TTL_SECONDS));',
         narration='The selector is the operating system user a process runs as. [[slnc 300]] The time to live is twenty seconds. [[slnc 300]] What the process says about itself is never part of the entry.'),
    dict(key='31-class-3', kind='code', title='Workload — the ledger reads the ID from the certificate',
         body='// Workload: the Java program each service runs\nparameters.setNeedClientAuth(true);\n...\ncaller = spiffeIdOf((X509Certificate) chain[0]);\n...\nint code = Policy.allows(caller, method, path) ? 200 : 403;',
         narration='The ledger demands a client certificate. [[slnc 300]] It reads the SPIFFE I D from the certificate, and applies the same rules as before.'),
    dict(key='32-class-4', kind='code', title='Workload — the client accepts one server ID',
         body='// Workload: the Java program each service runs\nreturn SpiffeSslContextFactory.getSslContext(SslContextOptions.builder()\n        .x509Source(source)\n        .acceptedSpiffeIdsSupplier(() -> Set.of(SpiffeId.parse(expectedId)))\n        .build());',
         narration="The source hands the current S V I D to every handshake, so rotation needs no code. [[slnc 300]] And the client accepts only the ledger's I D, which is why the fake ledger was refused."),
    dict(key='33-class-5', kind='code', title='Policy — anything not listed is refused',
         body='// Policy: which SPIFFE ID may call which route\nif (callerId == null || !callerId.startsWith(DOMAIN)) {\n    return false;\n...\nreturn RULES.contains(new Rule(callerId, method, path));',
         narration='An I D from another trust domain, or none, gets nothing. [[slnc 300]] Otherwise the rule for that I D, method and path must exist.'),
    dict(key='34-class-6', kind='code', title='SpiffeIds — trust domain, then path',
         body='// SpiffeIds: a teaching class: a SPIFFE ID taken apart by hand\nString rest = id.substring(SCHEME.length());\nint slash = rest.indexOf(\'/\');\nif (slash < 0) {\n    return new Parts(rest, "");\n}\nreturn new Parts(rest.substring(0, slash), rest.substring(slash));',
         narration='After spiffe colon slash slash comes the trust domain, up to the first slash, then the path. [[slnc 300]] A test checks that it agrees with the java spiffe library.'),
    dict(key='35-class-7', kind='code', title='Rotations — count the serial number changes',
         body='// Rotations: how often a certificate was replaced\nfor (int i = 1; i < seenInOrder.size(); i++) {\n    if (!seenInOrder.get(i).equals(seenInOrder.get(i - 1))) {\n        changes++;',
         narration='Every new certificate has a new serial number. [[slnc 300]] Counting the changes, call after call, counts the rotations.'),
    dict(key='36-outro', kind='outro', title='Thanks for Watching',
         body=['Full source, notes, diagrams and an animated walkthrough', 'are in the repository. Add a fraud-engine entry', 'that may only read statements.'],
         narration="That's workload identity with SPIFFE and SPIRE. [[slnc 400]] If you remember one sentence, make it this one. [[slnc 300]] Replace a shared secret with a name for each service, proven by a certificate the platform issues and renews every few seconds. [[slnc 500]] The full source code, written notes, diagrams, and an animated walkthrough are all in the repository. [[slnc 300]] It runs on your own machine. [[slnc 500]] Here is one exercise to try. [[slnc 300]] Add a registration entry for fraud engine that may only read statements, and call the ledger as it. [[slnc 500]] If you found this useful, a like really helps other people discover it. [[slnc 300]] Kindly subscribe to the channel. [[slnc 300]] It helps the channel grow and bring you more content. [[slnc 400]] Thanks for watching!"),
]

VIDEO = {'footer': 'Workload Identity  ·  Security Basics  ·  Java', 'poster': {'headline': [('WORKLOAD', 'text'), ('IDENTITY', 'accent')], 'taglines': [('Short-lived SPIFFE identities instead of a shared secret', 'gold')], 'before': {'code': 'X-Api-Key: <one key, 3 holders>', 'note': 'A leak is every service', 'tag': 'BEFORE'}, 'after': {'code': 'spiffe://bank.example/payments', 'note': '20 seconds, by registration', 'tag': 'AFTER'}}, 'highlight': {'console_green': ['THE REQUESTS (mutual', 'THE RESULT', 'THE SAME PROGRAM', 'AT THE SAME MOMENT', 'THE STOLEN SVID'], 'console_rose': ['THE LEAK', 'THE THEFT', "THE ATTACKER'S", 'TWO IMPOSTORS', 'THE SHARED SECRET IS'], 'console_amber': ['HOW LONG', 'ONE SPIFFE ID', "THE LEDGER'S AUDIT", 'THE PAYMENTS ENTRY'], 'console_cyan': ['THE SECRET', "THE LEDGER'S RULES", 'THE REQUESTS (the right', 'THE SETUP', 'THE REGISTRATION', 'THE TEST']}}
